## TaskRouter: Skills-based routing for contact centers

* [Activity Based Routing](/docs/taskrouter/activity-based-routing.md): Use TaskRouter activity-based routing to target or exclude Workers based on their Activity state, so you can route high-value Tasks and control Worker eligibility.
* [Activity Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/activity.md): Learn about the Activity resource, which describes the current state of your Workers, including Activity properties, and how to create, read, update, and delete Activities.
* [Event Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/event.md): TaskRouter logs Events for each state change in the Workspace, allowing you to build historical reporting and auditing for your routing use case.
* [TaskRouter Events reference](/docs/taskrouter/api/event/reference.md): Learn about TaskRouter Events and find descriptions of all Event Callbacks.
* [TaskRouter: REST API Reference](/docs/taskrouter/api.md): An overview of the TaskRouter API including the base URL for all TaskRouter resources and links to guides for best practices when building with this API.
* [Task Reservation Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/reservations.md): Full API reference for the Task Reservations resource in the TaskRouter API. Find sample code that shows how to fetch (get) and update reservations.
* [Task Channel resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/task-channel.md): A full API reference for the Task Channel resource, including code samples to create, fetch, read, update, and delete Task Channels.
* [Task Queue resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/task-queue.md): Full API reference for the TaskQueue resource in the TaskRouter API. Find sample code showing how to create, fetch (get), read (list), update, and delete.
* [Task Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/task.md): A TaskRouter Task represents a single item of work waiting to be processed. This overview covers task attributes, versions, and a Task lifecycle.
* [REST API: TaskQueue Statistics](/docs/taskrouter/api/taskqueue-statistics.md): Full API reference for the TaskQueue Statistics resource in the TaskRouter API. Sample code shows how to retrieve real-time, cumulative, and instance TaskQueue Statistics.
* [Worker Channel Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/worker-channel.md): Full API reference for the Worker Channels resource in the TaskRouter API. Sample code shows how to fetch (get), read (list), and update worker channels.
* [Worker Reservation resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/worker-reservation.md): Full API reference for the TaskRouter API's Worker Reservation resource. Sample code shows how to fetch (get), read (list), and update a WorkerReservation.
* [Worker Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/worker.md): Twilio TaskRouter allows you to create, update, delete, and retrieve Workers, which are entities that can perform tasks. A Worker might be an agent working in a call center, or a salesperson handling leads.
* [Worker Statistics Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/worker/statistics.md): Full API reference for the TaskRouter Worker Statistics resource. Code samples show how to fetch a resource and retrieve worker statistics.
* [Workflow Statistics Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/workflow-statistics.md): Learn about the Workflow Statistics resource, how to retrieve Workflow Statistics, RealTime Statistics, and Cumulative Statistics.
* [Workflow Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/workflow.md): Full API reference for the Workflow resource in the Taskrouter REST API. Sample code shows how to create, fetch, read, update, and delete a Workflow.
* [REST API: Workspace Statistics](/docs/taskrouter/api/workspace-statistics.md): Learn about the Workspace Statistics TaskRouter provides. Find sample code for how to retrieve statistics, real-time statistics, and cumulative statistics for Workspaces.
* [Workspace Resource](/docs/taskrouter/api/workspace.md): A full reference for the Workspace Resource of the TaskRouter API, with code samples to create, read, update, fetch, update, and delete a Workspace.
* [Call Control Concepts | Appendix | Web Sequence Diagrams](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint/call-control-concepts-web-sequence-diagrams.md): Web sequence diagrams for TaskRouter call control concepts, including blind (cold) transfer and warm transfer flows between caller, Twilio, and your app.
* [Call Control Concepts](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint/call-control-concepts.md): A conceptual overview of best practices for TaskRouter call control for reporting and dashboard accuracy, including holds, transfers, monitor, and barge.
* [Using Chat and TaskRouter together | Appendix | Web Sequence Diagrams](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint/chat-taskrouter-web-sequence-diagrams.md): Web sequence diagram for using Programmable Chat with TaskRouter, showing the flow from a customer starting a chat to an agent handling and completing the task.
* [Using Chat and TaskRouter Together](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint/chat-taskrouter.md): Learn best practices for using Programmable Chat with TaskRouter for Contact Center use cases.
* [Dashboards](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint/dashboards.md): Learn about common requirements for real-time dashboards for your TaskRouter contact center including data flow, summaries, queues, and agents.
* [Using Email and TaskRouter Together](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint/email-taskrouter.md): Learn how to add Email as a channel to your contact center, by combining a REST based third party email service with TaskRouter.
* [Contact Center Blueprint](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint.md): Learn how to build a contact center on Twilio. Reference code samples, architecture guidance, and design philosophies as you build out your contact center.
* [Reporting](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint/reporting.md): Common reporting requirements for a contact center, including real-time dashboards and historical reports to ensure you have all the data you need.
* [Using SMS and TaskRouter Together | Web Sequence Diagrams](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint/sms-taskrouter-web-sequence-diagrams.md): Web sequence diagram source for using SMS and TaskRouter together in a contact center. Copy the diagram code into websequencediagrams.com to customize the flow.
* [Using SMS and TaskRouter Together](/docs/taskrouter/contact-center-blueprint/sms-taskrouter.md): Learn the best practice for adding SMS as a channel to your contact center by combining Twilio Numbers, Programmable SMS, Programmable Chat, and TaskRouter.
* [Using TaskRouter Expressions](/docs/taskrouter/expression-syntax.md): TaskRouter uses a SQL-like expression syntax for binding Workers to TaskQueues and filtering Tasks into TaskQueues in a Workflow configuration. Learn how to compose expressions from  constants, JSON keys, as well as logical and comparison operators.
* [Assigning Tasks to Workers: Handling Assignment Callbacks](/docs/taskrouter/handle-assignment-callbacks.md): Learn how to handle Assignment Callbacks in TaskRouter, including parameters included in the HTTP POST request and ways to respond in your code.
* [How TaskRouter Works](/docs/taskrouter/how-taskrouter-works.md): Learn the basics of how TaskRouter, our system for distributing tasks in your contact center, works. Includes an example application flow and useful links.
* [TaskRouter: Skills-based routing for contact centers](/docs/taskrouter.md): TaskRouter is a skills-based routing system that provides the heart of a contact center that you can control from your code.
* [TaskRouter.js v1: Integrating TaskRouter to your browser-based applications](/docs/taskrouter/js-sdk-v1.md): Learn about integrating TaskRouter into your browser-based applications using the Javascript SDK.
* [TaskRouter.js v1: Constructing JWTs](/docs/taskrouter/js-sdk-v1/workspace/constructing-jwts.md): Generate JSON Web Tokens (JWTs) from your server to authorize which TaskRouter resources a client-side TaskRouter.js v1 application can access.
* [TaskRouter.js v1 Workspace: Managing resources in the browser](/docs/taskrouter/js-sdk-v1/workspace.md): The TaskRouter Javascript SDK allows developers to interact with the entire TaskRouter REST API by a simple JS API.
* [TaskRouter.js v1 TaskQueue: Managing a TaskQueue resource in the browser](/docs/taskrouter/js-sdk-v1/workspace/taskqueue.md): Learn how to manage a TaskQueue resource in the browser using the TaskRouter Javascript SDK.
* [TaskRouter.js v1 Worker: Managing workers in the browser](/docs/taskrouter/js-sdk-v1/workspace/worker.md): Comprehensive documentation about TaskRouter's JavaScript workers, used to route tasks in your web-based applications.
* [TaskRouter.js v2: Reconnect Logic](/docs/taskrouter/js-sdk-v2/reconnect.md): Documentation about how TaskRouter SDK v2 is reconnecting the WebSocket after disconnection.
* [TaskRouter.js v2 and v3: Integrating TaskRouter with your browser-based applications](/docs/taskrouter/js-sdk.md): Learn about integrating TaskRouter into your browser-based applications using TaskRouter JavaScript SDK v2 and v3.
* [Lifecycle of a Task: Task state](/docs/taskrouter/lifecycle-task-state.md): Learn the basics of the various states of a Task, the core of TaskRouter, including the states a Task can go through as it moves from pending to completed.
* [Lifecycle of a Task: Workflows and Assignment](/docs/taskrouter/lifecycle-task-workflows-and-assignment.md): Learn about the lifecycle of a TaskRouter Task, how it moves through a Workflow, and how it gets assigned to a Worker.
* [TaskRouter resource limits](/docs/taskrouter/limits.md): Twilio TaskRouter has default limits on certain resources, configuration parameters, and endpoints. If you are running into these limits, please reach out to our support team. We're happy to help work with you to increase some of these limits or help optimize how you use TaskRouter.
* [Upgrade to TaskRouter SDK version 2](/docs/taskrouter/migrate-js-sdk-to-v2.md): Migrate from TaskRouter JavaScript SDK version 1 to version 2: callbacks to promises, worker activity changes, event listening, and breaking changes.
* [Multitasking](/docs/taskrouter/multitasking.md): TaskRouter Multitasking allows a worker to handle multiple tasks of different types in parallel.
* [Ordering Workers](/docs/taskrouter/order-workers.md): Learn how to use the order\_by expression to designate in what order Workers are assigned tasks when using TaskRouter.
* [Queue Ordering](/docs/taskrouter/queue-ordering-last-first-out-lifo.md): Learn about how TaskQueue handles ordering with First In, First Out (FIFO) or Last In, First Out (LIFO).
* [TaskRouter .Net Quickstart](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp.md): In this Quickstart, build a simplified version of a multi-channel sales and technical support system using Twilio TaskRouter and .Net.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Accept a Reservation using Assignment Callback Instructions](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/reservations-accept-callback.md): Accept a TaskRouter Reservation automatically with an assignment callback instruction, by returning an accept instruction in a synchronous HTTP response from your C# server.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Accept a Reservation using the REST API](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/reservations-accept-rest.md): Accept or reject a TaskRouter Task Reservation by making an HTTP POST request to a Reservation instance resource with the TaskSid and ReservationSid in C#.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Set up the Assignment Callback URL](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/reservations-assignment-callback.md): Set up a TaskRouter Assignment Callback URL with a C# web server so TaskRouter can notify your application when a Worker is reserved to perform a Task.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Create a Task using the REST API](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/reservations-create-task-rest.md): Create a TaskRouter Task with the REST API in C#, then bring an eligible Worker online to trigger an automatic Task Reservation and assignment callback.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure TaskQueues](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/setup-add-task-queues.md): Add and configure TaskRouter TaskQueues that route Tasks to Workers by language skill, using SQL-like Target Workers expressions in the TaskRouter console.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure Workers](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/setup-add-workers.md): Add and configure TaskRouter Workers in the console, defining their skills with JSON attributes so TaskRouter can route Tasks based on language and other criteria.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure a Workflow](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/setup-add-workflow.md): Add and configure a TaskRouter Workflow with routing configurations that direct incoming Tasks to the correct TaskQueue based on the caller's selected language.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Create a Workspace](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/setup-create-workspace.md): Create a TaskRouter Workspace, the container element for any TaskRouter application, using the TaskRouter console or the REST API.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: Create a TaskRouter Task using \<Enqueue>](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/twiml-create-task.md): Create a TaskRouter Task from an incoming phone call in C# using the TwiML \<Enqueue> verb, routing the call to a Task Queue by selected language.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: Dequeue a Call to a Worker](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/twiml-dequeue-call.md): Dequeue a TaskRouter call to an eligible Worker in C#. Return a dequeue Assignment Instruction to bridge the caller to the Worker's contact\_uri.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: End-to-End Phone Call Task Assignment](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/twiml-end-to-end.md): Test the end-to-end TaskRouter phone call flow in C#: create a Task from an incoming call, route it to an available Worker, and bridge the call.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: Receive an Incoming Call](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/csharp/twiml-receive-call.md): Receive an incoming Twilio phone call in C# and prompt the caller for a language with TwiML \<Gather> before creating a TaskRouter Task.
* [Control Worker Activities using Worker.js: Add an Agent UI to our Project](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/agent-ui-add-project.md): Add a real-time agent user interface to a TaskRouter Java app using TaskRouter.js, letting Workers view and control their own Activity state.
* [TaskRouter Java Quickstart](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java.md): In this Quickstart, build a simplified version of a multichannel sales and technical support system using Twilio TaskRouter and Java.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Accept a Reservation using Assignment Callback Instructions](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/reservations-accept-callback.md): Accept a TaskRouter Reservation automatically by returning an 'accept' assignment instruction from your Assignment Callback URL, using Java and the REST API.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Accept a Reservation using the REST API](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/reservations-accept-rest.md): Accept a TaskRouter Reservation with the REST API by making a POST request to the Reservation instance resource with ReservationStatus set to accepted.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Set up the Assignment Callback URL](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/reservations-assignment-callback.md): Set up a TaskRouter Assignment Callback URL with a Java servlet so TaskRouter can notify your server when a Worker is reserved to perform a Task.
* [Creating Tasks and Accepting Reservations: Create a Task using the REST API](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/reservations-create-task-rest.md): Create a TaskRouter Task with the REST API using Java or curl, then bring an eligible Worker online to trigger an automatic Task Reservation.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure TaskQueues](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/setup-add-task-queues.md): Add and configure TaskRouter TaskQueues that route Tasks to Workers by language skill, using Target Workers Expressions with the HAS operator.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure Workers](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/setup-add-workers.md): Add and configure TaskRouter Workers in the console, defining JSON Worker Attributes such as language skills to route incoming Tasks by capability.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Add and Configure a Workflow](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/setup-add-workflow.md): Add and configure a TaskRouter Workflow with routing configurations that match incoming Tasks to the right Task Queue based on the caller selected language.
* [Setting up a TaskRouter Workspace: Create a Workspace](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/setup-create-workspace.md): Create a TaskRouter Workspace, the container element for any TaskRouter application, using the REST API or the TaskRouter console in the Twilio Console.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: Create a TaskRouter Task using \<Enqueue>](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/twiml-create-task.md): Create a TaskRouter Task from an incoming Twilio phone call using the TwiML \<Enqueue> verb in Java, routing the caller to a Task Queue based on their selected language.
* [Create Tasks from Phone Calls using TwiML: Dequeue a Call to a Worker](/docs/taskrouter/quickstart/java/twiml-dequeue-call.md): Use a TaskRouter dequeue Assignment Instruction in Java to connect an enqueued caller to an eligible Worker at their contact\_uri when the Worker becomes available.
